\f1\fs22 The Airborne, as a rule, preferred their feet exactly where evolution never intended: firmly planted in nothing but expensive air. However, even the most dedicated sky-dweller occasionally craved the primitive pleasure of actual ground beneath their manicured toes. For this reason, the Airborne had claimed the city's most spectacular harbourside real estate, and to keep the sub-Layer toxic micro climate at a kilometres-long arm\'92s length, thedeployed bubble technology over and around the harbour side that would make a soap enthusiast weep with envy.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>These weren't your garden-variety bubbles. The Harbour Enclave's protective dome was a triumph of privileged otherwise ordinary Ulyssian engineering, simultaneously dispersing the Layer above while maintaining a selective permeability that made velvet ropes look welcoming. Try to enter without the proper genetic markers, credit rating, 
\f2\i haute couture
\f1\i0  or ancestral documentation dating back at least six generations, and the bubble would politely but firmly redirect you into the harbuor. The rejection came with a complimentary towel, because the Airborne weren't barbarians.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The crown jewel of this terrestrial playground was The Domain, eighty acres of meticulously maintained parkland that sat to the east of the city centre like a green reproach to urban planning. Originally designated the Governor's private farm\'97because nothing says "colonial administration" like claiming the best land for your personal vegetable patch\'97it had evolved through various incarnations before achieving its current status as the Airborne's premier outdoor venue.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The Domain's history read like a comedy of administrative errors. Early on,, it had been a public ground where commoners were graciously allowed to breathe the same air as their betters, provided they didn't breathe too loudly. By the early 20th century, it hosted speakers' corners where radicals shouted about workers' rights while the Airborne's ancestors flew overhead, dropping investment portfolios like propaganda leaflets.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The Bubble Era had transformed The Domain into something more exclusive. The great fig trees, descendants of flora that had watched convicts become investment bankers, now filtered air that cost roughly twelve credits per breath. The sandstone garden walls, built by historical unfortunates who couldn't afford ??antigrav??, served as quaint reminders of when people had to use their legs for everything.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Modern amenities included:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0\cf2 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>	\'95	Self-maintaining lawns that judged your shoes before allowing you to step on them\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>	\'95	Heritage-listed trees fitted with discrete speakers that whispered stock prices\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>	\'95	Ornamental ponds stocked with fish genetically modified to swim in aesthetically pleasing patterns\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>	\'95	Viewing platforms that offered spectacular harbour vistas to the north while automatically editing out any evidence of the Pale to the southwest\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The Domain's event spaces were legendary among those who could afford to know they existed. The lawns could contain up to fifty thousand thousand people for outdoor concerts and festivals\'97though the Airborne rarely gathered more than fifty, as crowds were considered d\'e9class\'e9. The Pavilion, a structure that somehow managed to be both temporary and eternal, hosted charity galas where the wealthy gathered to feel better about wealth.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The booking process for The Domain required forms that themselves required forms. The application questionnaire included such pertinent queries as:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0\cf2 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>	\'95	"Will your event maintain appropriate elevation standards?"\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>	\'95	"Do any of your guests suffer from altitude deficiency?"\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>	\'95	"Rate your commitment to atmospheric superiority on a scale of Stratospheric to Merely Tall"\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The grounds crew consisted entirely of descendants of the original gardeners, as The Domain's charter specified that only those with hereditary chlorophyll appreciation could tend its lawns. They moved through the gardens like green-thumbed ghosts, visible only when someone stepped on a flower bed, at which point they materialised with the sorrowful disappointment of saints witnessing sin.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The irony, lost on no one except the Airborne themselves, was that The Domain represented the city's last democratic space\'97if democracy was defined as "equally excluding everyone who mattered equally little." The bubble overhead shimmered with the perpetual promise that yes, you too could enjoy this pastoral paradise, provided you could afford the kind of bubble key that came with its own bubble key butler.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>For the Elvi to propose holding a concert here wasn't just audacious\'97it was like suggesting that gravity was optional. The Domain had hosted many events over its centuries of existence: military parades, royal visits, cultural festivals celebrating cultures the Airborne had never experienced. But never had it faced the prospect of amplified rockabilly at volumes that could make sandstone question its life choices.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The Domain waited, its lawns innocent of what was to come, its trees unaware they would soon vibrate to "Hound Dog" in B-flat, its heritage-listed everything about to discover that evolution, when given a sequined jumpsuit and a Marshall stack, didn't knock politely at the door.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>It kicked it down and sang about it.\

